  "dom_id": "step-q349765",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A concert venue is tracking the rate at which guests enter the building. The rate $r(t)$ at which guests arrive is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\sqrt{t+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nBetween $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, to one decimal place,\n{{field-1}} guests arrived.",
  "worked_solution": "We're given the **rate of change** in the number of guests, $r(t).$ To find the number of guests that arrived between the times $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}3\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =3\\,\\frac{2(t+1)^{3/2}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2(3+1)^{3/2}-2(0+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =2(4)^{3/2}-2(1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=0$ and $t=3$ minutes, $14$ guests arrived.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
